diff --git a/manual/cli-tooling/profiles.md b/manual/cli-tooling/profiles.md
index 57c784fb..fe370131 100644
--- a/manual/cli-tooling/profiles.md
+++ b/manual/cli-tooling/profiles.md
@@ -101,6 +101,16 @@ lbx shared.lbox close
 ```
 
 You can also use `open --password-stdin` or `open --password-env VARIABLE_NAME`. Explicit password opening caches access in the Session Agent without creating or unlocking a local Vault. It supports reading and extraction without platform secure storage. A password Profile does not supply an owner signing key; writing still requires the appropriate signing material.
+Password opening admits at most **32 KDF attempts per password credential** by
+default, shared across primary, mirrored and backup candidates. This bounds work
+on an untrusted access directory without changing which archives are valid.
+For an independently trusted archive that needs more attempts, explicitly use
+`open --password-slot-budget ATTEMPTS`; the same option applies to stored
+password Profiles. Each locally configured Profile password receives its own
+allowance, so this is not a global cap across all Profiles. A zero allowance
+refuses password derivation. Larger allowances deliberately permit more CPU and
+memory work. Explicit budget APIs are also available in the Rust and language
+bindings; their existing open methods retain the default allowance.
 
 If the server loses its password, retrieve it again from your Vault. If the Profile is lost, restore its backup. If both are lost, another identity that can manage the Lockbox can grant a replacement password; the original password cannot be recovered from the Lockbox itself.
 
diff --git a/manual/cli-tooling/revault-session-agent.md b/manual/cli-tooling/revault-session-agent.md
index 1b8c6d09..a67f2f06 100644
--- a/manual/cli-tooling/revault-session-agent.md
+++ b/manual/cli-tooling/revault-session-agent.md
@@ -15,7 +15,7 @@ The agent:
 * caches content keys for open Lockboxes;
 * expires inactive entries after their time to live;
 * extends an entry's expiry when it is used;
-* clears cached keys when the machine starts to suspend;
+* clears cached keys when its active watcher receives a suspend notification;
 * can prevent sleep while a secret operation is active; and
 * can terminate an active secret operation if the machine nevertheless suspends.
 
@@ -42,7 +42,18 @@ agent.prevent_sleep = true
 agent.terminate_on_suspend = true
 ```
 
-`prevent_sleep` asks the operating system to remain awake while a sensitive operation is active. `terminate_on_suspend` stops registered reVault operations if suspension cannot be prevented. Cached content keys are cleared on a suspend request in either case.
+Both settings default to `true`. `prevent_sleep` requests an operating-system sleep inhibitor during sensitive work. `terminate_on_suspend` requires a ready local suspend watcher before the operation accesses secrets; the process terminates itself when notified, without asking the agent to signal a numeric PID. If requested protection is unavailable, the operation fails with an actionable error.
+
+The agent clears cached keys when its active watcher receives a suspend notification. `lockbox session` and `lockbox doctor` report whether that watcher is active. An unavailable watcher does not provide suspend cleanup; cache expiry and explicit session closure remain available. Notifications and inhibition depend on operating-system facilities and cannot guarantee protection against forced suspend or notifications that the system does not deliver.
+
+For deliberately unprotected headless automation without a system bus or supported power service, explicitly disable both policies:
+
+```bash
+export LOCKBOX_AGENT_PREVENT_SLEEP=false
+export LOCKBOX_AGENT_TERMINATE_ON_SUSPEND=false
+```
+
+This opt-out removes suspend inhibition and local suspend termination; it does not make cached keys safe across sleep when monitoring is unavailable. Prefer short sessions and close them with `lockbox session stop` after use. These settings are read when the agent starts: stop an existing agent before changing them, then start the next session with the intended environment.
 
 Environment variables can override these values:
 
